results.problems:
No strays: git status --porcelain before staging showed only src/chevt3.c, src/chevt3.h, tests/chevt3.c and tools/code_emit/data/emit_state.json, all inside the pipeline's own paths. No separate stray commit was needed.

One bookkeeping correction worth flagging. The emitter wrote the in_flight entry at the TOP LEVEL of tools/code_emit/data/emit_state.json, as a sibling of "_doc" and "functions", rather than inside the "functions" object where all 148 other entries live. The pre-existing diff was literally:
    },
  +  "00037af0": {
  +    "status": "in_flight",
  +    "in_flight_since": "2026-09-01 06:15"
    }
  }
Carrying it forward verbatim in that position would have produced a "committed" record that next_batch.py never reads, so the address would be handed out again on a later run as though it had never been emitted. I moved the entry into "functions" and removed the stray top-level key; the resulting diff is a single clean hunk appending 00037af0 after 000379d0 with status "committed", emitted_against "0x7a", rounds 0, and no in_flight_since. No other entry and no other field was touched, and "_doc" is unchanged. Worth checking the emitter's writer for the same off-by-one-level bug on future functions.

Both verdict files carry ghidra_fixes: [] and open_issues: [] as expected, so nothing was written to Ghidra, the baseline audit and snapshot re-export were correctly skipped (ghidra_gate_clean is false only because the gate was not run, not because it failed), and nothing was appended to emit_issues.json — issues_logged is 0.

Also noted while checking, not acted on: tests/chevt3.c defines run_chevt3_tests, which is referenced nowhere outside the file itself. The same is true of run_chevt2_tests in the already-committed tests/chevt2.c, and the gate reported 99 chevt3 checks inside a 7114-check suite, so the runner list is evidently generated by the build rather than kept as a tracked registry file. No registry file needed updating.
